roll-dih8.11 met the 150 MB budget of docs/RUST-PORT.md Appendix A for replaying 200,000 events on the heap: `cargo run --release --package xtask -- timing --replay 200000 [--ownership]` counts at most 107 MB held at once on the plain history and 133 MB on the ownership-heavy one (2026-10-12, this Mac). The resident peak `/usr/bin/time` reports for the same process is about 185 and 210 MB. The system allocator keeps most of what replay frees (each event's operation once applied, the records the journal no longer needs) for sizes the state it builds never asks for again, so the resident set is about the journal at load plus everything replay allocates. The user's decision of 2026-10-12 (roll-dih8.11) made the heap the budget and sent this gap here.

Ways to close it, none tried:
- free the journal's memory in whole blocks: its records built in an arena per segment, which goes once replay has taken what it needs;
- hold less at once: apply events as they are read instead of loading the journal first (replay needs the causal order first); the local projection (roll-lqjj.2) makes a whole-journal replay the exception rather than every command's cost;
- another allocator that returns freed memory sooner (mimalloc), a new dependency with C code.

Studio reads trackers through beadroll-core. The work lands in pancake-studio; this issue tracks it. It needs only Phase 1. The plan was docs/RUST-PORT.md §7, removed in roll-dih8.24; this description carries it, corrected (2026-10-13): the repository is public, decision 7 is taken (below), and the export is State::export_records().

Today studio reads the committed .beads/issues.jsonl from a shallow bare clone of an operator-approved branch: pancake-studio-beads parses it into an immutable graph, the worker stores a normalised projection in Postgres, the HTTP side queries it inside spawn_blocking, and refreshes poll the branch tip every 120 s. bead publishes its tracker on a coordination ref, normally refs/beads/journal, so studio needs a persisted source migration as well as a reader. The surfaces: crates/pancake-studio-core/src/source.rs; migrations/20260909080058_project_identity.sql (the existing constraint; change it through a new migration); crates/pancake-studio/src/sources/{reader,worker,policy}.rs; crates/pancake-studio-db/src/sources.rs (source revisions and baseline resets).

What studio's schema and policy already fix (checked at studio 31cfafa): one source per project; approval per host; the revision rule is also a database trigger (studio_source_revision()); the reader is branch-shaped (CanonicalRef, clone --branch and discovery are branch-only; a refresh reads one ref and one blob, and its unchanged check compares one tip); limits stricter than bead's (10,000 issues, JSON depth 64, titles of 500 characters, 16 MiB of input; bead nests to 100 and has no title cap; a tracker beyond them is rejected whole, by studio's all-or-nothing rule); publications admit one input profile, beads-rust-jsonl-v1, and store the input bytes; pancake-studio-core/AGENTS.md states that persisted source refs are refs/heads/....

Bridge:
- keep the approved code branch as a separate configuration source. Resolve its .beadroll.json ref (default refs/beads/journal) and remote (default the code endpoint) into a persisted tracker binding: approved coordination endpoint, ref, expected workspace and credential binding. Studio holds one source per project (studio_project_sources.project_id is the primary key; refresh cycles, publications and approval receipts record one repository, ref and commit), so the binding gets its own row or columns with its own access revision, and provenance records both the configuration commit and the journal commit. Decide whether the tracker ref lives in canonical_ref;
- the coordination remote may be a distinct repository. Approval is per host (authorize_remote admits any repository on an enabled endpoint) and some credential scopes cover a host, so the tracker endpoint, ref and expected workspace are set only by an operator's source command, never from .beadroll.json: a refresh whose .beadroll.json names another endpoint, ref or workspace fails and changes nothing. Explicitly reject unsupported remote-name and local-path forms;
- if the tracker ref lives in canonical_ref, migrate its refs/heads-only constraint as well as CanonicalRef; either way replace the clone --branch prefix assumption with explicit arbitrary-ref fetching, extend the studio_source_revision() trigger along with sources.rs, and update the refs/heads invariant in pancake-studio-core/AGENTS.md. A refresh reads two refs and a whole tree within the byte budget, and its unchanged check compares both tips;
- migrate existing publications and later endpoint/ref/expected-workspace changes through Studio's source revision mechanism: increment content generation, clear the ancestry baseline and obsolete trust/admission state, and reject stale refresh work. Extend the current repository/ref comparison to expected workspace. Credential-only changes use the existing access-revision mechanism. An unrelated journal root must never be checked as a descendant of the old code branch;
- shallow trees suffice for replay, but ancestry verification retains the existing fetch/deepen loop;
- it passes the tree's entries to beadroll_core::published::Tree::read with the expected workspace (which refuses a tree without a readable manifest, of another workspace or in another format) and replays tree.journal() with beadroll_core::replay::replay;
- it feeds State::export_records(), written as Beads JSONL, to the existing pancake-studio-beads parser; the normalised graph and member-output contracts stay the same, and its br-0.5.7 fixtures describe that export format;
- acceptance covers migrating an existing code-branch publication and two successive journal refreshes, including ancestry that needs deepening; code and coordination in distinct repositories; rejection of unapproved redirection, unsupported remote forms and workspace mismatch; later binding changes with obsolete refresh work rejected; and a tracker beyond studio's limits, rejected whole.


Native: map State straight onto studio's normalised graph and drop the JSONL round trip; this needs a second publication input_profile and its migration. Studio keeps its own limits, all-or-nothing validation, privacy (design, notes, comments, labels and assignee never reach member output), canonical agreement and plan digests; beadroll provides typed, exact text.

Build:
- studio depends on beadroll-core as a git dependency on the public repository (github.com/bpcakes/beadroll), pinned to one commit: the maintainer's decision 7 of 2026-10-10 (docs/REVIEWING.md §2). No build needs credentials (studio's image build carries no secret by rule, and its CI fetches git dependencies anonymously), no vendoring, and the crates stay publish = false;
- it moves its toolchain pin from 1.97.1 to 1.99 in the same change, with its image builder's FROM rust:1.97.1 base, which refuses a compiler that differs from the pin; sha2 0.10, thiserror 2 and indexmap 2 are already in its graph.

Execution runs (not part of this issue): agents in devbox worktrees run bead instead of br. The unmerged execution-runs branch diffs the worktree's .beads/issues.jsonl against the base commit's export every 30 seconds, and with bead neither file exists: its runner must read the published ref (or the local store) and diff by event, each change attributed to an agent thread and a branch.

Anyone moving a Beads project to bead needs to know, before anything is written or published, what `bead init --import FILE` (or `bead import FILE`) would do. Found while planning a multi-project migration (2026-10-13), kept general: every point below applies to any Beads repository.

`--dry-run` on `bead import FILE` and on `bead init --import FILE`: reads and checks FILE exactly as the import would (read_records, then plan_import against the tracker's state), writes nothing, creates no store, asks no remote, and reports:
- whether the import would go through (`importable`), and if not, every problem it would be refused for, not only the first (e.g. every Beads memory record, `_type: memory`, which has no id: roll-dih8.16), as the import's own messages;
- how many records it holds, how many are new and how many this tracker already has (left as they are: an import never updates a known issue), the new ones by status;
- the new `in_progress` records, which arrive with no owner (a claim in bead) and need a decision each;
- dependencies on keys neither the tracker nor the file has (kept by key until such an issue arrives);
- in a git checkout, where the tracker has changes the checkout lacks: each local or remote-tracking branch with commits touching `.beads/issues.jsonl` that HEAD does not have, and each linked worktree with uncommitted changes to it. Beads keeps its tracker in a branch-tracked file, so importing one checkout's copy silently misses work recorded on other branches, and a later import adds only issues, never another copy's newer closes or edits.

`init --dry-run` plans against the local tracker if this checkout already has one, else an empty one, and says that the remote was not asked (a tracker it already publishes would be joined, only the records it lacks imported). `--dry-run` without `--import` on init is a usage error. A dry run exits 0 whatever its verdict; `importable` says whether the import would go through.
